Sprint review and retrospective


Applying roles 

When working on the sprint, every role on the Scrum-Agile team was a major part of the project being successful. The product owner was responsible for describing and emphasizing the backlog, making sure that the team put its focus on working on high-value features first. The product owner was responsible for explaining the requirements that were needed for the user authentication feature. This was useful because it helped the developers in the process of building what was required. The scrum master was responsible for assisting in the sprint ceremonies and getting rid of any problems that were a hindrance to the testing team completing their work. The developers collaborated by sharing reviews of the code that they were working on to ensure that there were no errors.

Completing User Stories.

The Scrum- Agile Approach played an important role in completing user stories. For example, the user story for executing the travel itinerary feature was tested and developed within a single print. This was important because it allowed developers to give feedback in a quick timeframe. A great example of the Scrum agile approach is the user story, allowing users to filter results on the type of flights, keywords like price, and distance.








Handling Interruptions

When there were interruptions within the project that slowed down progress, that's when the scrum framework demonstrated that it was a useful and effective approach. Around the middle of the development stage, there was a new requirement that involved making changes in data handling and user authentication. What's great is that the project was able to succeed because of the agile process. The agile process was instrumental in giving the tools that the product owner needed. The product owner was able to make changes that were needed to the backlog fast and efficiently without any problems.


Communication


The team would not be successful if everyone didn't communicate. I would say that this was the most important part. The scrum master was helpful because communication among the different teams made the development process go a lot smoother. For example, the developers and the testers worked together to solve a problem that was related to the software. By both teams working together, they were able to come up with a solution in a relatively short time. Another form of communication that was helpful was giving everyone an email to remind the team the the tasks that need to be completed and when the deadlines were.




Organizational tools

The team used a variety of organizational tools and followed the Scrum principles closely. The team made use of JIRA because it was useful for managing sprint planning, product backlog, and keeping track of progress. The tools were useful and ensured that there would be a high standard of quality, and any bugs or errors would be resolved before the final stages.





Evaluating Agile process pros and cons


The pros were that it provided the flexibility that was needed to adapt to changing environments. Another benefit was that it enhanced communication among team members and promoted collaboration. The cons were that there was a significant backlog of changes and required extensive training, and some employees might not have received proper Scrum training.




Describing the Waterfall model.

The waterfall model comes with advantages and disadvantages. The disadvantages are that it doesn't provide flexibility, you cant make any changes that may be needed during a project. Another disadvantage is that it's not suitable for large, complex projects. The advantages are that it's easy to manage and has a clear structure that makes it simple for the user to learn.





I think that the Agile approach was the best because it provided a lot of benefits that were indeed in the completion of the project. It allowed there to be transparency, which I think is very important, and it came with flexibility and the ability to make changes to the backlog when needed, which you cant do in the waterfall methodology.
